{smcl}
{* *! version 1.1.0 02oct2026}{...}
{cmd:help dagamesolve}
{hline}

{title:Title}

{p2colset 5 19 21 2}{...}
{p2col:{hi:dagamesolve}{hline 2}} Solve finite N-player discrete-action games for Nash equilibria{p_end}
{p2colreset}{...}


{title:Syntax}

{p 8 14 2}
{cmd:dagamesolve} {varlist} {ifin}{cmd:,}
{cmdab:pay:offs(}{varlist}{cmd:)}
{cmdab:group(}{varname}{cmd:)}
{cmdab:eq:uilibria(}{it:stub}{cmd:)}
[{opt fast}
 {opt random:points(#)}
 {opt pure:only}
 {opt noisy}
 {opt tol:erance(#)}
 {opt mb:width(#)}
 {opt col:tol(#)}
 {opt digit:s(#)}
 {opt mn:its(#)}]


{title:Description}

{pstd}
{cmd:dagamesolve} solves finite, simultaneous-move, discrete-action games of
complete information. It supports N-player games in which players may have
different numbers of actions and can solve either a single game or a sequence
of games in one call.
{p_end}

{pstd}
By default, {cmd:dagamesolve} searches for pure-strategy and mixed-strategy
Nash equilibria, including equilibria in which some players mix while others
play pure strategies. The mixed-strategy search uses interval methods,
contraction and subdivision, followed by Newton refinement. The default
procedure is intended to provide a systematic search for all equilibria of
the finite game.
{p_end}

{pstd}
Because mixed-strategy equilibrium conditions form systems of nonlinear
equations, computation can become demanding as the numbers of players and
actions increase. The {cmd:fast} option provides a substantially faster
Newton-based search, while {cmd:pureonly} restricts attention to
pure-strategy equilibria.
{p_end}

{pstd}
Games are supplied in "list" form. Each observation within a game corresponds
to a player. Each variable in the main {varlist} corresponds to one complete
pure-strategy action profile, and the corresponding variable in
{cmd:payoffs()} contains each player's payoff under that profile.
{p_end}

{pstd}
The variable specified in {cmd:group()} identifies games, not players. Within
each game, observations identify the players by their ordering. When several
games are solved in one call, they should have the same number of players and
the same action structure, with corresponding player observations ordered
consistently across games.
{p_end}

{pstd}
{cmd:equilibria()} specifies a stub for the variables created by
{cmd:dagamesolve}. For example, {cmd:equilibria(e)} creates variables beginning
with {cmd:e_}.
{p_end}


{title:Required options}

{phang}
{cmd:payoffs(}{varlist}{cmd:)}
specifies the payoff variables. The payoff variables must correspond one-for-one
and in the same order with the action-profile variables supplied in the main
{varlist}.
{p_end}

{phang}
{cmd:group(}{varname}{cmd:)}
specifies the variable identifying each game. Observations within each group
represent players.
{p_end}

{phang}
{cmd:equilibria(}{it:stub}{cmd:)}
specifies the stub used to name the generated equilibrium variables. The
necessary output variables must not already exist.
{p_end}


{title:Options}

{phang}
{cmd:fast}
uses a faster Newton-based search for mixed-strategy equilibria rather than the
default interval-search procedure. Under the default method,
{cmd:dagamesolve} uses interval arithmetic, contraction, and subdivision to
identify candidate regions before refining solutions with Newton iteration.
With {cmd:fast}, this interval-search stage is skipped and Newton iteration is
instead started from randomly selected points in the mixed-strategy simplex.
This can substantially reduce computation time, especially for larger games,
but it does not provide the same systematic search of the strategy space as
the default method and may therefore fail to locate some mixed-strategy
equilibria.
{p_end}

{phang}
{cmd:randompoints(}{it:#}{cmd:)}
specifies the number of randomly selected starting points used by the
{cmd:fast} method. The default is {cmd:50}. For each subgame considered during
the mixed-strategy search, starting values are drawn from the appropriate
mixed-strategy simplices and Newton iteration is applied from those points.
Increasing {it:#} gives the Newton search more opportunities to locate distinct
equilibria but increases computation time. This option is relevant only when
{cmd:fast} is specified.
{p_end}

{phang}
{cmd:pureonly}
restricts the search to pure-strategy Nash equilibria. No mixed or partially
mixed equilibria are sought. This can be useful when only pure-strategy
solutions are of interest or when a quick preliminary analysis is desired.
{p_end}

{phang}
{cmd:noisy}
displays progress information while games are being solved. During the
mixed-strategy search, dots are printed as subgames are processed, and summary
information is displayed when a game has been completed.
{p_end}

{phang}
{cmd:mbwidth(}{it:#}{cmd:)}
controls the target maximum width of interval boxes used by the default
complete-search method. The default is {cmd:1e-4}. During the interval-search
stage, candidate regions of the mixed-strategy space are represented by boxes
and repeatedly contracted or subdivided. Smaller values of {it:#} require the
interval search to continue with finer boxes before candidate regions are
passed on for Newton refinement. This may tighten candidate neighborhoods but
can substantially increase computation time. Because Newton iteration is
subsequently used to refine candidate solutions, extremely small values are
often unnecessary.
{p_end}

{phang}
{cmd:tolerance(}{it:#}{cmd:)}
sets the convergence tolerance used in Newton iteration. The default is
{cmd:1e-12}. Newton iteration stops when its convergence criterion is satisfied
at this tolerance, subject also to the maximum number of iterations specified
by {cmd:mnits()}. Smaller values impose a stricter convergence requirement and
may increase computation time or make convergence more difficult in poorly
conditioned systems.
{p_end}

{phang}
{cmd:coltol(}{it:#}{cmd:)}
sets the tolerance used to determine whether numerically obtained candidate
solutions should be treated as the same equilibrium. The default is
{cmd:1e-4}. Candidate solutions that are sufficiently close according to this
criterion are consolidated rather than reported as separate equilibria.
Smaller values make consolidation less aggressive; larger values make it more
aggressive. This option can matter when distinct equilibria are close together
or when numerical noise generates nearly duplicate candidate solutions.
{p_end}

{phang}
{cmd:digits(}{it:#}{cmd:)}
controls the precision parameter used in interval-arithmetic calculations.
The default is {cmd:1e-8}. Interval arithmetic uses outward rounding so that
floating-point error does not incorrectly exclude values that may occur within
an interval. Smaller values of {it:#} correspond to finer outward rounding.
This setting is distinct from {cmd:tolerance()}, which controls Newton
convergence, and {cmd:coltol()}, which controls consolidation of candidate
solutions.
{p_end}

{phang}
{cmd:mnits(}{it:#}{cmd:)}
sets the maximum number of Newton iterations allowed for each candidate
starting value or interval region. The default is {cmd:100}. Newton iteration
may stop earlier when the convergence criterion specified by
{cmd:tolerance()} is satisfied.
{p_end}


{title:Data organization}

{pstd}
Consider the following two-player, two-action game. Player 1 chooses rows,
player 2 chooses columns, and player 1's payoff is listed first:
{p_end}

        {txt}              Player 2
        {txt}              1        2
        {txt}{hline 31}
        {txt}Player 1  1 |  1,2      0,.1
        {txt}          2 |  0,0      3,1
        {txt}{hline 31}

{pstd}
The four pure-strategy action profiles can be written in list form as
{p_end}

        player 1:    1   1   2   2
        player 2:    1   2   1   2

{pstd}
and the corresponding payoff variables are
{p_end}

        player 1:    1   0   0   3
        player 2:    2  .1   0   1

{pstd}
Thus the four action-profile variables and four payoff variables line up
column by column.
{p_end}

{pstd}
The utility command {help dagamestrats} automates creation of the action-profile
variables.
{p_end}


{title:Example 1: A two-player game}

{pstd}
The preceding game can be entered directly as follows:
{p_end}

{phang}{cmd:. clear}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. set obs 2}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. gen id = 1}{p_end}

{phang}{cmd:. gen a1 = 1}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. gen a2 = 1}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. replace a2 = 2 in 2}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. gen a3 = 2}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. replace a3 = 1 in 2}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. gen a4 = 2}{p_end}

{phang}{cmd:. gen pay1 = 1}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. replace pay1 = 2 in 2}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. gen pay2 = 0}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. replace pay2 = .1 in 2}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. gen pay3 = 0}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. gen pay4 = 3}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. replace pay4 = 1 in 2}{p_end}

{pstd}
Solve the game:
{p_end}

{phang}{cmd:. dagamesolve a1-a4, group(id) payoffs(pay1-pay4) equilibria(e)}{p_end}

{pstd}
This game has two pure-strategy equilibria and one completely mixed
equilibrium.
{p_end}


{title:Output variables}

{pstd}
If {cmd:equilibria(e)} is specified, equilibrium strategy probabilities are
stored in variables of the form
{p_end}

        {cmd:e_}{it:q}{cmd:_}{it:p}{cmd:_}{it:a}

{pstd}
where {it:q} identifies the equilibrium, {it:p} identifies the player, and
{it:a} identifies that player's action.
{p_end}

{pstd}
For example,
{cmd:e_2_1_3}
contains the probability that player 1 assigns to action 3 in equilibrium 2.
{p_end}

{pstd}
The equilibrium strategy probabilities are stored on the final observation of
each game identified by {cmd:group()}. This convention allows the complete
equilibrium profile for a game to be represented in one observation.
{p_end}

{pstd}
Variables of the form
{cmd:e_rets_}{it:q}
contain players' expected payoffs in equilibrium {it:q}. Because observations
within a game represent players, these return variables contain the appropriate
player-specific expected payoff on each observation in the game.
{p_end}

{pstd}
Finally,
{cmd:e_count}
contains the number of equilibria found for each game. The count is stored on
the final observation of the game.
{p_end}


{title:Example 2: A sequence of games}

{pstd}
{cmd:dagamesolve} can solve many games with the same player and action structure
in one call. The following creates three four-player games in which each player
has two actions:
{p_end}

{phang}{cmd:. clear}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. set obs 12}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. gen id = ceil(_n/4)}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. gen acts = 2}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. dagamestrats acts, group(id) generate(s)}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. scalar profiles = r(profiles)}{p_end}

{pstd}
Generate random payoffs for each action profile:
{p_end}

{phang}{cmd:. set seed 5150}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. forvalues i = 1/`=profiles' {c -(}}{p_end}
{phang}{txt:  2. }{cmd:gen pay`i' = rnormal()}{p_end}
{phang}{txt:  3. }{cmd:{c )-}}{p_end}

{pstd}
Then solve all three games in one call:
{p_end}

{phang}{cmd:. dagamesolve s*, group(id) payoffs(pay*) equilibria(eq) noisy}{p_end}

{pstd}
The resulting variables {cmd:eq_*} contain equilibrium strategies, equilibrium
payoffs, and equilibrium counts separately for each game.
{p_end}


{title:Example 3: Faster or restricted searches}

{pstd}
To use 200 random Newton starting values instead of the default interval search:
{p_end}

{phang}{cmd:. dagamesolve s*, group(id) payoffs(pay*) equilibria(ef) fast randompoints(200)}{p_end}

{pstd}
The {cmd:fast} solution may be much quicker, but it should not be interpreted as
having the same completeness properties as the default interval search.
{p_end}

{pstd}
To search only for pure-strategy equilibria:
{p_end}

{phang}{cmd:. dagamesolve s*, group(id) payoffs(pay*) equilibria(ep) pureonly}{p_end}


{title:Numerical considerations}

{pstd}
Mixed-strategy equilibrium conditions for finite games form systems of
multivariate polynomial equations. These systems may contain multiple roots,
closely spaced roots, or poorly conditioned regions. Complete numerical
solution of such systems can therefore be computationally expensive.
{p_end}

{pstd}
The default mixed-strategy procedure uses interval methods to search candidate
regions of the strategy space and Newton iteration to refine candidate
solutions. Interval methods are generally more computationally demanding than
starting Newton iteration from a collection of point guesses because they also
attempt to exclude regions in which no solution can lie.
{p_end}

{pstd}
The numerical options should therefore be changed with care. In particular,
{cmd:tolerance()}, {cmd:coltol()}, and {cmd:digits()} control different aspects
of the computation: Newton convergence, consolidation of nearby solutions, and
interval rounding, respectively. Changing these values can affect both
computation time and how numerical candidate solutions are represented.
{p_end}

{pstd}
The {cmd:fast} option is useful when speed is especially important, but because
it relies on Newton searches from randomly chosen initial points, it may miss
equilibria whose basins of attraction are not reached by the selected starting
values.
{p_end}


{title:Stored results}

{pstd}
{cmd:dagamesolve} stores the following in {cmd:r()}:
{p_end}

{synoptset 24 tabbed}{...}
{synopt:{cmd:r(playeractions)}}row vector containing the number of actions available to each player{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(totalactions)}}total number of player-actions in the game{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(method)}}solution method: {cmd:complete} or {cmd:newton}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(games)}}number of games solved{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(meaneqs)}}mean number of equilibria found across games{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(maxeqs)}}maximum number of equilibria found in any game{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(mineqs)}}minimum number of equilibria found in any game{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(sdeqs)}}standard deviation of equilibrium counts across games{p_end}

{pstd}
When the default complete method is used, {cmd:dagamesolve} additionally stores
{p_end}

{synoptset 24 tabbed}{...}
{synopt:{cmd:r(mbwidth)}}interval-box width setting{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(coltol)}}solution-consolidation tolerance{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(mnits)}}maximum number of Newton iterations{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(digits)}}interval-rounding precision parameter{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(tolerance)}}Newton convergence tolerance{p_end}

{pstd}
When {cmd:fast} is used, {cmd:dagamesolve} additionally stores
{p_end}

{synoptset 24 tabbed}{...}
{synopt:{cmd:r(randomeqpoints)}}number of random Newton starting points{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(mnits)}}maximum number of Newton iterations{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(tolerance)}}Newton convergence tolerance{p_end}


{marker rems}{title:Remarks}

{pstd}
The computational burden of equilibrium search grows rapidly with the number
of players and actions because both the number of pure-strategy profiles and
the number of possible mixed-strategy supports increase quickly.
{p_end}

{pstd}
Consequently, the default complete search may become slow for games of moderate
or large size. This is a consequence of the attempt to search systematically
for mixed-strategy equilibria rather than relying only on point-based root
finding.
{p_end}

{pstd}
{cmd:dagamesolve} is under active development. Numerical results from difficult
games should be inspected carefully, particularly when equilibria are very
close together or when alternative numerical settings lead to different
reported solution counts.
{p_end}


{title:Dependencies}

{pstd}
{cmd:dagamesolve} requires {help moremata}, {help int_utils},
{help rowmat_utils}, and {help intsolver}. The repository's
{cmd:dependency.do} file installs the required packages.
{p_end}

{pstd}
The package uses the Mata library {cmd:ldagamesolve.mlib}. Source code for the
Mata routines is included in the project's {cmd:Support} directory.
{p_end}

{pstd}
Source code, tests, and development materials are available from the
{browse "https://github.com/mbaker21231/DaGameSolve":DaGameSolve GitHub repository}.
{p_end}


